A||||4||FIEROCIOUS||Exhaust springs nessasary or not?||||1||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||08-17-2000||02:52 PM||||I recently changed my exhaust and installed the rear muffler springs. (The ones we all LOVE so much.) Didn't have too much trouble installing them.<P>My exhaust has been hitting the front subframe for quite a while now, and still does. I think that if I ditch the springs the exhaust will hang lower and not hit the subframe.<P>My question is: Can you use the hangers that hold the tail pipes (87 GT) to hold the exhaust or is it nessasary to use the springs? <P>I'm stuck between a subframe and a swaybar please help! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-17-2000||04:10 PM||||You need to support the catalyst with something or you'll break the exhaust in short order. It doesn't have to be the springs.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||08-17-2000||04:47 PM||||I'll probably keep the front four exhaust springs but not use the six springs that hold the muffler in place.<P>I wonder if the springs do more to center the muffler than they do to hold it up?<P>On the 87 GT's the exhaust is also supported by rubber hangers typical to 70's cars, I was considering buying new rubber and hanging the exhaust a 1/4" lower. <P>Has anyone not used the springs?<P>Thanks for the help Ogre! <p>[This message has been edited by FIEROCIOUS (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-18-2000||02:02 PM||||you have the same issue with the muffler. It's allot of weight and needs hangers near or on it. The existing springs do support it and likely keep it centered but that's not their main job. Unless the rubber hangers you are talking about are within few inches of the muffler they are probly to far away. You could actually cause cracking of the pipe if they are to far out on the tail pipe(s).<P>You might want to look at the frame brakets. I don't know about all of them but the one I looked at was bolted to the frame. You might could drill a differant hole in them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||08-18-2000||02:53 PM||||Hey, Ogre fixed it today! I contemplated and sat figureing, finaly I got off my ace and played with it a bit. (The exhaust people!)<BR>the area were it was hitting was the front cross member pasenger side were it loops to give the exhaust system room. <P>Well, I ended up tweeking the exhaust a bit by sticking a 2x4 in between the frame and the exhaust were it touched and then at the rear of the car as it goes up to the muffler I used another 2x4 and a jack and straitened the pipe. <BR>I gained aprox. 1/4 inch clearance and all is good not rubbing or rattles!<P>Thanks for the help again!||||reg||1
